const editorial = {
  heading: "Why the music died at your last party — and the cheap fix nobody explains",
  html: `
<p>Every host has a version of the same story. The food is out, the yard is full, somebody asks you to turn it up — and the speaker you brought starts to fall apart. The reviews for cheap party speakers tell it in the owners' own words: <em>"can't go above 50% volume, speaker starts to distort, and it now has a constant crackle regardless of volume."</em> <em>"They have zero depth, zero bass, and really aren't that loud."</em> <em>"Dies after like 1 1/2- 2hrs."</em></p>

<p>Here is the part almost nobody gets told: that speaker didn't fail because it was cheap. It failed for a specific, physical, fixable reason — and once you know it, the whole category sorts itself into keepers and junk in about thirty seconds.</p>

<h3>One driver, two jobs</h3>

<p>Open up a $30 party speaker and you find one small full-range driver doing everything. Bass and treble, simultaneously, at volume, outdoors. Bass is the problem: producing it takes large physical movement — excursion — and a small driver runs out of travel fast. Push it past that point and the waveform clips. That clipping is the crackle. It is not wear, and it is not bad luck. It is one component being asked to do two jobs.</p>

<figure class="ed-figure">
  <img src="/images/walla-ed-drivers.jpg" alt="Two-way speaker driver layout showing a separate woofer and tweeter" width="1400" height="1045" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
  <figcaption>Two drivers, two jobs: the woofer takes the excursion-hungry bass, the tweeter takes the highs.</figcaption>
</figure>

<p>The fix has been standard in serious audio for seventy years: split the work. A dedicated woofer takes the bass, a dedicated tweeter takes the highs, and neither is ever pushed past what it can do. A JBL owner reviewing his own speaker put it better than we could: <em>"I fully attribute this to the added tweeter, which eases the burden of the main driver from producing all frequencies."</em> That is a customer, unprompted, explaining the exact mechanism.</p>

<p>The Boom+ runs that architecture — a 15W woofer and a 5W tweeter, stated plainly on the maker's own spec page — at a price where most of what you'll find is still one lonely driver doing two jobs. That, and not any adjective, is why it holds together at the volume where the speaker you currently own gives up. <a href="${WALLA_LINK}" onclick="checkdirect(${CPV_CAMPAIGN},1)">See the full spec on the #1 card</a>.</p>

<h3>The battery number on the box is not the battery number in your yard</h3>

<p>The second way a party dies is quieter: the speaker just stops. And here the whole category — famous names included — has a rating problem. The JBL Charge 6 is rated at 24 hours; SoundGuys measured it at <strong>13 hours and 15 minutes</strong> at realistic volume in their standardised June 2025 test. Owners are blunter: <em>"I do not know how they claim 15 hours. I get 3-4 hours outdoors with full volume."</em> <em>"This one? 2 hours and the red light is blinking."</em></p>

<p>We can't fix the industry's ratings. What we can do is refuse to play the game: every battery figure on this page is printed as a <em>rating</em>, attributed to its maker, and where an independent measurement exists we cite it — including when it counts against the brands we rank. The Boom+ is rated 8 hours. Treat that the way you should treat every number in this category, and it covers a BBQ that starts at four and ends in the dark.</p>

<h3>So why not just buy the JBL?</h3>

<p>Let's take the strongest version of the objection, because you will have it: a JBL Flip 7 discounts to about a hundred dollars, it has a real woofer-and-tweeter design too, it carries an IP68 certification, and it is a genuinely excellent small speaker. We ranked it on this page and said so.</p>

<p>Here is what the badge doesn't change: physics. Outdoors there are no walls to reflect sound. Every doubling of distance from a single speaker costs you about six decibels — so one box, <em>any</em> one box, gives you a loud circle of chairs and a quiet everywhere-else. The people at the far table aren't hearing your speaker, whatever logo is on the grille.</p>

<p>The fix isn't a louder box. It's two boxes, spread apart, playing true left and right. And this is where the price sheet stops being boring: <strong>a pair of Boom+ speakers runs about $174. A pair of Flip 7s lists at $259.90. One Charge 6 lists at $199.95 — and it's still mono.</strong></p>

<p>That is the entire argument of this page in three numbers. The market's own words for how a challenger brand earns the money: <em>"Best $70 speaker you can get imo. Competes with the jbl extreme3."</em> Nobody talks a friend into a spec sheet. They talk them into a comparison — and this one you can check from your phone in ninety seconds. <a href="${WALLA_LINK}" onclick="checkdirect(${CPV_CAMPAIGN},1)"><strong>Check today's 2-pack price →</strong></a></p>

<h3>What we'd skip, and who should buy something else</h3>

<p>If one speaker has to carry a genuinely big, loud space on its own — a tailgate, a block party — buy wattage: that is rank 3's job, and it does it at rank 3's size and price. If your speaker will actually go <em>in</em> the water rather than beside it, buy a certified IP rating; the Boom+ doesn't publish one and we're not going to pretend otherwise. And if you just want something for the kitchen bench, the cheap option at rank 5 is honestly enough.</p>

<p>Everyone else — the birthday in the backyard, the pool afternoon, the deck at dusk — is who this ranking is for.</p>

<h3>Buy one, or buy two</h3>

<figure class="ed-figure">
  <img src="/images/walla-ed-backyard.jpg" alt="Two portable speakers placed at opposite ends of a backyard gathering" width="1400" height="1037" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
  <figcaption>Coverage beats wattage: two paired units, twenty feet apart, and there is no quiet end of the yard.</figcaption>
</figure>

<p>One Boom+ covers the patio. The reason to look hard at the 2-pack is what happens at the second press of a button: the units pair into true stereo — a left and a right, not the same mono signal twice — and the per-unit price drops to about $87. An owner, after doing exactly this: <em>"I love it so much I purchased a second one to pair with the first one I bought and it was even better."</em></p>

<p>Ninety days of no-questions money-back applies either way. That window exists because the maker knows exactly what an unknown brand has to prove, and it is three times what the store gives you. <a href="${WALLA_LINK}" onclick="checkdirect(${CPV_CAMPAIGN},1)"><strong>See single and 2-pack pricing →</strong></a></p>

<h3>The next party is already on the calendar</h3>

<p>You know the moment this page exists to delete: the food out, the yard full, the volume up, and that first electrical crackle in front of everyone. The speaker that does that is doing it because of what's inside it, and it will do it again next time.</p>

<p><strong>Four of these five speakers ask you to pay for a single box and hope it reaches the back fence. One is priced so you can stop hoping — and put a speaker at both ends.</strong> <a href="${WALLA_LINK}" onclick="checkdirect(${CPV_CAMPAIGN},1)"><strong>Check availability and today's price →</strong></a></p>
`,
};

